body:
The latest update now consumes the user-gesture when requestStorageAccess is denied (due to no TLD interaction).
This is now forcing the user to click a second time to open a popup to our TLD. Could we make it so that the rejection is still part of the user-gesture in this case, to allow a popup in the same click?

```
  document.requestStorageAccess().then(() => {
    // Storage access was granted.
    // Check whether the user is logged in.
    // If not, do a popup to log the user in.
  }, () => {
    // Storage access was denied.
    // --> If due to not having TLD interaction, then open a popup
    //     to allow the user to interact  <---- Same user-gesture
  })
```

@othermaciej and @annevk: We are meeting with Mozilla within a week or two to discuss this API among some other things. We will cover the slight differences between our two implementations, developer feedback, and a joint set of platform tests. I'll make sure to mention the PR.

@williamchong007: In the specific case of Safari, the third party still has access to partitioned localStorage. That might change, but it's still the case. Note that partitioned localStorage is not persistent. We worry a bit on users being pushed if third-parties would know that they didn't allow storage access.

@kushal Yes, we are still discussing it and it's on the agenda for our meeting with Mozilla. But I think it should be hashed out in a separate issue and not here. If/once such an API is available, the Storage Access API can refer to it.

@johnwilander, it would be nice to see some recommendations for a good UX pattern to provide for when users interact with an iframe that calls `requestStorageAccess` and it fails because the user has not visited the site in a first party context before (or within the appropriate time frame). Having to show a pop up (to avoid the user losing context of what's going on) with some UI element for them to interact with can feel very similar to a malware experience. What is a better way?

For example, we're using the Storage Access API to make a polyfill work for the Credential Handler API, and we wind up having to explain the anti-tracking feature itself so that users understand why they are being asked to jump through hoops. You can see this if you go here in Safari and click "Install" and then "Allow" on the modal that comes up (this modal is rendered in an iframe):

https://credential-repository.demo.digitalbazaar.com/
